    "description": "# Future directions\n\nThis project formalizes the *order-theoretic bridge* connecting structural\ngraph/matroid theory (Robertson\u2013Seymour forbidden minors) with commutative\nalgebra (Dickson's lemma / finite generation of monomial ideals). The single\ntheorem `WQOFiniteBasis.exists_finite_generating_family` \u2014 every upper set of a\nwell-quasi-order is generated by its finite antichain of minimal elements \u2014\nspecializes to both, and its dual `exists_finite_forbidden_family` is the\nabstract Robertson\u2013Seymour corollary.\n\nFiles:\n- `Catalog/Novelty/ExcludedMinors/WellQuasiOrderFiniteBasis.lean` \u2014 the abstract\n  WQO finite-basis engine (Robertson\u2013Seymour / Dickson bridge).\n- `Catalog/Novelty/ExcludedMinors/ZnGain.lean` \u2014 a concrete, fully proved\n  excluded-minor instance: over every cyclic group `\u2124/n` (`n \u2265 2`) the\n  parallel-class slice of the Zaslavsky/Funk conjecture holds with single\n  excluded minor `(n+1)K\u2082` (`digon_excluded_minor`), together with the\n  divisibility monotonicity law `gainable_mono_of_dvd`. This realizes, in one\n  concrete family, the \"explicit minor order + forbidden minor\" pattern that the\n  abstract engine describes in general.\n\n## Natural next steps\n\n1. **Instantiate to an explicit minor order on matroids.** Build a `PartialOrder`\n   on (isomorphism classes of) matroids from the minor relation, so that\n   `robertson_seymour_of_wqo` applies literally to a Lean `Matroid` type. The\n   hard input \u2014 that a given class is WQO \u2014 would remain a hypothesis, but the\n   forbidden-minor corollary would then be a genuine matroid statement.\n\n2. **Formalize the graph-minor `PartialOrder`.** Similarly, equip finite\n   `SimpleGraph`s (up to isomorphism) with the minor order and derive the finite\n   forbidden-minor characterization of any minor-closed graph property, *given*\n   the Robertson\u2013Seymour WQO hypothesis.\n\n3. **Dickson \u2192 Hilbert basis theorem.** Connect `dickson` to Mathlib's monomial\n   ideals: show the minimal-generator set produced here is a generating set of\n   the corresponding `MonomialIdeal`, recovering finite generation as a corollary\n   and linking to Gr\u00f6bner-basis infrastructure.\n\n4. **Higman's lemma.** Extend from the finite product `Fin k \u2192 \u2115` to the WQO of\n   finite words / `List` over a WQO alphabet (Higman's lemma). Mathlib already\n   has some `PartiallyWellOrderedOn` machinery for subsequences; combining it\n   with the finite-basis engine here would give finite obstruction sets for\n   subword-closed languages.\n\n5. **Kruskal's tree theorem** as a further WQO input, feeding the same\n   finite-obstruction corollary for tree-minor-closed families.\n\n6. **Effective / decidable versions.** For decidable minor tests and finite\n   ambient data, turn the abstract finite `Set` of obstructions into a concrete\n   `Finset`, yielding a decision procedure schema for membership in a\n   minor-closed class.\n\n## Scope note\n\nThe deep theorems (Robertson\u2013Seymour itself; Geelen\u2013Gerards\u2013Whittle WQO for\n`F_q`-representable matroids) are the *hypotheses* consumed by the bridge, not\nits conclusions. The contribution here is the reusable engine that converts any\nsuch WQO input into the finite forbidden-minor / finite-generation output, made\nfully formal and axiom-clean in Lean 4 + Mathlib.\n",
