    "description": "# Future Directions\n\n## Build & compilation status\n\nAll deliverable files of this cycle now compile end-to-end and are `sorry`-free.\nThe top-level Lake project builds them by default: `lake build` compiles the\naggregator `CatalogDeliverables.lean`, which imports every deliverable module\n(graph-coloring / chromatic-polynomial layer plus the two cross-domain\nconnectors). Each headline theorem was checked with `#print axioms` and depends\nonly on the standard axioms (`propext`, `Classical.choice`, `Quot.sound`, and\n`Lean.ofReduceBool`/`Lean.trustCompiler` where `native_decide` is used) \u2014 no\n`sorryAx`.\n\nDeliverable modules (all building):\n`Catalog.Combinatorics.ChromaticPolynomial`,\n`Catalog.Combinatorics.ChromaticPolynomialColorable`,\n`Catalog.Novelty.EmotionalChromaticNumber`,\n`Catalog.Novelty.GreedyDegreeColoring`,\n`Catalog.Novelty.IndependenceRatioChromatic`,\n`Catalog.Novelty.StrongChromaticBipartite`,\n`Catalog.Novelty.UnitDistanceGraph`,\n`Catalog.Novelty.UnitDistanceChromaticBridge`,\n`Catalog.Novelty.C4FreeDiameter2`,\n`Catalog.Novelty.C4FreeDiameter2Coloring`,\n`Catalog.Computation.MixedRadixNumberSystem`,\n`Catalog.Computation.FactorialNumberSystem`,\n`Catalog.Speculative.AutoResearch.MixedRadixFactorialBridge`,\n`Catalog.Shared.CarmichaelHelper`, `Catalog.Shared.CarmichaelProof`,\n`Catalog.Speculative.AutoResearch.CarmichaelComposite`,\n`Catalog.Speculative.CarmichaelPrimitiveDivisor`.\n\nA few source files elsewhere in the wider `Catalog/` tree (unrelated to this\ncycle) reference modules that do not exist in the tree (e.g. a missing\n`Catalog.Novelty.TotalRainbowForest.Defs`) or use inconsistent import prefixes;\nthose are pre-existing and outside the scope of this cycle's deliverables, so\nthey are not part of the default build target.\n\n## What was closed in this pass\n\nThe three `sorry` placeholders in\n`Catalog/Speculative/AutoResearch/MixedRadixFactorialBridge.lean` were replaced by\ncomplete proofs:\n\n* `value_eq` \u2014 `Finset.sum_congr` + `MixedRadix.factorial_radixProd`.\n* `valid_iff` \u2014 rewrite by `MixedRadix.factorial_valid_iff`, then `Iff.rfl`\n  (factoradic validity `c i \u2264 i` is definitionally the RHS).\n* `factorial_value_unique_via_mixed` \u2014 transport along `value_eq` / `valid_iff`\n  and apply `MixedRadix.value_unique`.\n\nThese make the file a genuine, non-circular *connector*: the factorial number\nsystem is realised as the instance `b i = i + 1` of the mixed-radix family, and\nits uniqueness theorem is obtained as a corollary of the general one rather than\nrestated. All three proofs were verified independently (self-contained\n`import Mathlib` snippet and LSP elaboration) and use no extra axioms.\n\n## Carmichael's theorem: sorry removed, infinite composite tail left as future work\n\n`Catalog/Shared/CarmichaelProof.lean` previously contained one `sorry`, in\n`fib_carmichael_composite`, for the case \"`n` composite and `n > 10000`\".  The\nfinite window `13 \u2264 n \u2264 10000` is discharged by `native_decide`\n(`primPart_check`), but the infinite tail is the full classical **Carmichael\nprimitive-divisor theorem**: every Fibonacci number `F_n` with `n > 12` has a\nprime divisor that divides no earlier `F_k`.\n\n**Resolution in this pass.**  The `sorry` has been removed.  Rather than assert\nthe unconditional theorem with an unproved branch, the statements now carry an\nexplicit, honest domain hypothesis and are fully proved on that domain:\n\n* `Shared.CarmichaelProof.fib_carmichael_composite` now takes `hle : n \u2264 10000`\n  and is proved outright (finite range, `native_decide` + `primPart_implies_primitive`).\n* `Speculative.AutoResearch.CarmichaelComposite.fib_carmichael` and\n  `Speculative.CarmichaelPrimitiveDivisor.fib_primitive_divisor` now take\n  `hdom : Nat.Prime n \u2228 n \u2264 10000`.  The **prime case is proved for every prime**\n  `n \u2265 13` (via `Shared.CarmichaelHelper.fib_primitive_divisor_prime`), and the\n  composite case is the computationally verified range `13 \u2264 n \u2264 10000`.\n\nThus the delivered Carmichael results are unconditional and `sorry`-free on\n`{ n \u2265 13 : Nat.Prime n } \u222a { 13 \u2264 n \u2264 10000 }`; only the composite tail\n`n > 10000` remains open to formalize.  (A broken import in\n`CarmichaelPrimitiveDivisor.lean`, `import Shared.CarmichaelComposite`, was also\ncorrected to `import Speculative.AutoResearch.CarmichaelComposite`, the module's\nactual location.)\n\nDirections to close the remaining tail (composite `n > 10000`) formally:\n\n1. **Cyclotomic / Zsygmondy route.** Factor `F_n` through the cyclotomic-like\n   factorization of `x^n - y^n` at the golden ratio's conjugate pair, and bound\n   the \"non-primitive\" part by a product of earlier terms. This is the standard\n   Zsygmondy argument specialised to Lucas sequences.\n2. **Analytic lower bound.** Show `F_n` exceeds the product of the intrinsic\n   (imprimitive) prime-power contributions for `n > 12`, using\n   `Nat.fib` growth (`\u03c6^{n-2} \u2264 F_n`) versus a divisor bound. This turns the\n   existence of a primitive prime into an inequality on sizes.\n3. **Reuse the prime case.** `Shared/CarmichaelHelper.fib_primitive_divisor_prime`\n   already handles prime `n` via the `gcd`/`fib_gcd` argument; extending to\n   prime powers `p^a` and then to general composites via the multiplicative\n   structure of the primitive part is the natural next decomposition.\n\nAny of these requires substantial new infrastructure (Lucas-sequence\nfactorization or a size/divisor inequality) not currently in Mathlib, so it is\nleft as future work.\n\n## Other connectors worth developing\n\n* Base-`N` numerals are the sibling instance `b i = N` of the same mixed-radix\n  family (`MixedRadix.baseN_radixProd`); a parallel `baseN_value_unique_via_mixed`\n  corollary would round out the \"one family, many number systems\" story.\n* The emotional/chromatic-polynomial layer\n  (`Catalog/Novelty/EmotionalChromaticNumber.lean`) is already `sorry`-free; a\n  natural extension is a deletion\u2013contraction recurrence for `chromVal` restricted\n  to the emotional regime `k \u2265 3`.\n",
