    "description": "# Future Directions\n\nThis project formalizes a rigorous fragment of the \"Galois theory of reversible\ncellular automata\" programme, working with binary CAs on the cyclic lattice\n`\u2124/n` (file `Reversibility.lean`). What is proved:\n\n* **Counting.** `256` elementary rules, `8` neighbourhoods.\n* **Translation invariance (Hedlund-type).** Every elementary global map commutes\n  with the shift (`caMap_shiftFun_comm`).\n* **The six distinguished reversible rules.** Wolfram rules `204, 51, 170, 240,\n  15, 85` identified as identity / complement / left-shift / right-shift and their\n  complements, each proved bijective.\n* **An irreversible rule.** The constant rule is not bijective on any non-trivial\n  lattice.\n* **Reversibility group.** The centraliser of the shift in `Equiv.Perm (Config n)`;\n  the shift and the complement belong to it.\n* **Group structure.** Complement is an involution, the shift has order dividing\n  `n`, the two commute, and the subgroup they generate is abelian.\n* **Exact order of the shift.** On any non-trivial cyclic lattice the shift has\n  order *exactly* `n` (`shift_orderOf`), proved via `shift_pow_ne_one`: for\n  `0 < k < n` the `k`-fold shift moves the point-mass configuration at index `0`.\n\n## Natural next steps\n\n1. **Structure of `\u27e8shift, complement\u27e9`.** Upgrade \"abelian\" to an explicit\n   isomorphism `\u27e8shift, complement\u27e9 \u2245 (ZMod n) \u00d7 (ZMod 2)` (for `n \u2265 1`), via the\n   surjective homomorphism `(ZMod n) \u00d7 (ZMod 2) \u2192 Perm(Config n)` sending\n   `(a,b) \u21a6 shift^a * complement^b` and computing its kernel.\n\n2. **Classification of reversible elementary CAs.** Formalize that on the *bi-infinite*\n   line `\u2124` (or for all cyclic lattices simultaneously) the reversible elementary\n   binary CAs are exactly the six affine single-site rules \u2014 a genuine\n   Hedlund/Richardson-flavoured classification. This needs a formal statement of\n   the global map on `\u2124 \u2192 Bool` and a proof that surjectivity forces single-site\n   dependence.\n\n3. **Larger radius and alphabet.** Generalize `caMap` to radius `r` and alphabet\n   `Fin k`, and study the group generated by shifts and alphabet permutations.\n   The correct replacement for the mission's `S_{k^{2r+1}}` conjecture is likely a\n   wreath-product / affine-group description rather than a full symmetric group.\n\n4. **Inverse-is-a-CA (Richardson's theorem).** Prove that the inverse of a\n   reversible CA is again a CA (of some radius), which makes the reversible CAs a\n   genuine group under composition once radius is allowed to grow.\n\n## Note on the original conjecture\n\nThe mission's specific numerical claim `|G(1,{0,1})| = 8!/4 = 10080` is false\n(see `ComputationalEvidence.md`): the centraliser of the neighbourhood 3-rotation\nin `S\u2088` has order `36`, and reversible global maps are far more constrained than\narbitrary permutations of neighbourhoods. The theorems here capture the true\nunderlying structure instead.\n",
