    "description": "# Future Directions: Algebraic Geometry of Neural Decision Boundaries\n\nThis cycle established that a rectified-linear classifier's decision boundary is\na *tropical hypersurface*, and that its algebraic complexity is governed by two\nexact arithmetic laws of the max-plus algebra: monomial counts **add** under a\nrectified-linear activation and **multiply** under composition of independent\nunits. From these findings we distil the following bold, testable conjectures.\n\n## 1. Exact monomial-count recursion for layered networks\n\n**Conjecture.** For a layered rectified-linear network of depth `L` with layer\nwidths `w_1, \u2026, w_L`, the number of affine monomials in the numerator of the\ncomputed tropical rational function satisfies the exact recursion\n`m_{k+1} = m_k + d_k`, where `d_k` is the current denominator count, and hence is\nbounded above by `2^L \u00b7 \u220f_i w_i`; moreover this bound is attained on an open set\nof weights.\n\n**The key insight is** that a rectified-linear activation applied to a tropical\nrational `p \u2296 q` returns `max(p,q) \u2296 q`, so the numerator's monomial set is the\n*disjoint union* of the numerator and denominator sets \u2014 turning depth into\naddition and width into multiplication of monomial counts.\n\n**Why now?** The two combining laws (union under activation, product under\ncomposition) have been isolated as clean, weight-independent identities, so the\nrecursion can now be tracked exactly rather than estimated, and the tightness of\nthe classical `2^L` region bound can be tested layer by layer on trained\nnetworks.\n\n## 2. The singular locus is a codimension-two tropical stratum\n\n**Conjecture.** The non-smooth points of the decision boundary \u2014 where three or\nmore affine pieces meet \u2014 form a tropical stratum of codimension two, and their\nnumber is bounded by `\u220f_i (w_i choose 2)`.\n\n**The key insight is** that a boundary point is singular exactly when the maximum\ndefining the tropical polynomial is attained by three or more monomials\nsimultaneously, an incidence condition of the same combinatorial type that\ngoverns the vertices of tropical curves.\n\n**Why now?** Having characterized the boundary as the equalizer `{p = q}` of two\ncontinuous convex tropical polynomials, the singular locus can be described\npurely as a multiplicity-of-argmax condition, making the codimension count a\ncombinatorial question about which monomial families tie.\n\n## 3. Curvature-free certified robustness radius\n\n**Conjecture.** Because each tropical polynomial is convex and piecewise linear,\nthe certified robustness radius at a correctly classified point equals the\nclassification margin divided by twice the sum of the two dominant monomial\nslopes \u2014 with no dependence on any second-order (curvature) term.\n\n**The key insight is** that convexity forces the local Lipschitz constant to be\nrealized by a single affine piece, so the robustness certificate depends only on\nthe active monomial's weights, not on the global network.\n\n**Why now?** With convexity and continuity of the tropical value established, the\nlocal-to-global reduction needed for a tight, curvature-free certificate is\navailable, and can be benchmarked against gradient-based robustness bounds.\n\n## 4. Depth versus width: an addition/multiplication trade-off law\n\n**Conjecture.** At a fixed total monomial budget `B`, the class of decision\nboundaries expressible by a depth-`L` width-`w` network is strictly richer when\nthe budget is spent on depth (`B \u2248 2^L`) than on width (`B \u2248 \u220f w_i`), because\naddition of monomial families under activation produces non-convex boundaries\nunreachable by a single convex tropical polynomial.\n\n**The key insight is** that width alone builds a single convex tropical\npolynomial, whereas depth introduces subtraction (`p \u2296 q`) and thus genuine\ntropical *rationality*, escaping convexity.\n\n**Why now?** The precise split between the additive (activation) and\nmultiplicative (composition) growth mechanisms makes the depth/width expressivity\ngap a concrete, falsifiable comparison rather than a heuristic slogan.\n",
