    "description": "# Future Directions \u2014 Cancellation structure of spectral heat-kernel expansions\n\nThis cycle sharpened the leading-order cancellation theory in two directions:\na **moment-spectrum** characterisation (`L \u2261 0` iff all power sums `\u2211\u1d62 d\u1d62 E\u1d62\u1d4f`\nvanish) and a **dimension formula** for the cancellation space\n(`n \u2212 #{distinct energy levels}`), both traced to the invertibility of a\nVandermonde system on the distinct energy values. The following conjectures push\nthat structure further.\n\n## 1. Sub-leading term cancels within a fixed degeneracy pattern\n\n**Conjecture.** Fix the pattern of degeneracies (the partition of levels into\nequal-energy blocks). Whenever the leading correction cancels, the *next* order\ncorrection `\u2211\u1d62 d\u1d62\u00b2 e^{-tE\u1d62}` (second-order Feynman\u2013Hellmann) cancels for all `t`\nif and only if the second-order shifts also balance block-by-block, with a\ncancellation space of dimension `n \u2212 #{distinct levels}` \u2014 the *same* count.\n\n**The key insight is** that each order of perturbation theory sees the spectrum\nonly through the same level-aggregation map, so its cancellation freedom is a\nfixed invariant of the degeneracy pattern, independent of the order.\n\n**Why now?** The leading order has just been reduced to a single linear map\nwhose kernel dimension is exactly the degeneracy count; the natural next test is\nwhether that map controls all higher orders uniformly.\n\n## 2. Minimal sampling suffices\n\n**Conjecture.** For a spectrum with exactly `m` distinct levels, the leading term\ncancels for *all* `t` as soon as it cancels at any `m` distinct sample points\n`t\u2081, \u2026, t_m`. Equivalently, `m` measurements pin down the entire cancellation.\n\n**The key insight is** that the level-projected function lives in an\n`m`-dimensional space of exponentials, so `m` independent evaluations already\ndetermine it \u2014 the transcendental identity is finite-dimensional in disguise.\n\n**Why now?** The Vandermonde pivot used here already shows that natural-number\nsamples `t = 0,1,2,\u2026` suffice; compressing this to the minimal `m` samples is the\nsharp, experimentally testable version.\n\n## 3. Degeneracy is detectable from the cancellation dimension alone\n\n**Conjecture.** The dimension of the leading-term cancellation space is a\ncomplete invariant of the *coarse* spectral degeneracy: two spectra share the\nsame cancellation dimension for every perturbation class if and only if they have\nthe same number of distinct levels, regardless of the level values themselves.\n\n**The key insight is** that the dimension formula `n \u2212 #{distinct levels}`\ndepends on the spectrum only through its cardinality of distinct values, so the\ncancellation space forgets everything else about the energies.\n\n**Why now?** With the exact dimension count established, the degeneracy count\nbecomes an observable one can read off from cancellation phenomena rather than\nfrom the spectrum directly.\n\n## 4. Weighted / density-of-states generalisation\n\n**Conjecture.** Replace the counting of levels by a positive weight `w\u1d62` (a\ndensity of states). The weighted leading term `\u2211\u1d62 w\u1d62 d\u1d62 e^{-tE\u1d62}` cancels for all\n`t` iff every *weighted* level sum vanishes, and the cancellation space still has\ndimension `n \u2212 #{distinct levels}` \u2014 the weights rescale but never change the\ndegeneracy count.\n\n**The key insight is** that positive weights are an invertible diagonal change of\nvariables on the shift vector, so they preserve the kernel dimension of the\nlevel-aggregation map exactly.\n\n**Why now?** The unweighted map is now understood as a surjection onto the space\nof levels; conjugating by a positive diagonal is the smallest deformation that\ntests robustness of the whole picture.\n",
