    "description": "# Future Directions\n\n`Logic/ProofSearchInformation.lean` formalizes the counting core of the\ninformation-theoretic limits of encoding and proof search: no code compresses\nevery message, most messages are incompressible, and the candidate space of a\ngiven description length is exponential. The file now also includes the\nquantitative fraction bound `incompressible_fraction`, proving over `\u211a` that for\nslack `c \u2264 n` the fraction of messages with description length `\u2265 n - c` is at\nleast `1 - 2^{-c}`. Natural extensions:\n\n1. **Kraft's inequality.** Add prefix-free codes over `List Bool` and prove\n   `\u2211 2^{-|c(x)|} \u2264 1`, connecting the counting bound to expected code length and\n   Shannon's source-coding theorem.\n\n2. **Prefix-free description length = Kolmogorov complexity.** Instantiate the\n   `BinStr` description model with a concrete universal decoder and recover the\n   standard incompressibility theorem `K(x) \u2265 |x| - c` for most `x`.\n\n3. **Proofs as descriptions.** Replace the abstract `BinStr` code with an encoding\n   of Lean/first-order proof terms and state the search lower bound directly for\n   proof objects, making the \"find vs. verify\" gap explicit for a real proof\n   calculus.\n\n4. **Average-case over random theorems.** Formalize a distribution on statements\n   and prove the average description length is `\u0398(n)`, matching the mission's\n   conjecture that random statements have no short certificate.\n",
