    "description": "# Future Directions \u2014 The Combinatorics of Register Allocation\n\nThis cycle established that for programs whose variables occupy contiguous live ranges, the\ninterference graph is an interval graph, and its chromatic number, clique number, and maximum\nlive-range overlap coincide: the optimal number of registers is exactly the maximum number of\nsimultaneously live variables, achieved by a single left-to-right scan. The following bold,\ntestable conjectures extend that finding.\n\n## 1. Spill minimisation is governed by the overlap profile, not the graph\n\n**Conjecture.** When the register budget `k` is below the maximum overlap `D`, the minimum\nnumber of variables that must be spilled equals the total \"excess area\" of the overlap\nprofile: `\u2211_t max(depth(t) \u2212 k, 0)` counted with multiplicity of maximal over-full runs, and\nan optimal spill set can be chosen greedily by repeatedly evicting a variable that spans a\ndeepest over-full point.\n\nThe key insight is that spilling on an interval graph is a one-dimensional covering problem:\neach over-full program point must lose enough variables, and because live ranges are\nintervals, a single well-chosen eviction relieves an entire contiguous congested region at\nonce. Why now? The exact `\u03c7 = \u03c9 = maxDepth` identity turns the spill question into a purely\ngeometric one about the depth function, removing the graph-theoretic obfuscation that made\nearlier spill heuristics appear ad hoc.\n\n## 2. Degree-based spilling is provably within a constant factor of optimal\n\n**Conjecture.** Evicting the maximum-degree vertex of the interference graph repeatedly (the\nclassical \"degree-based\" heuristic) produces a spill set whose cost is at most twice the\noptimum on interval graphs, and this factor is tight.\n\nThe key insight is that on interval graphs a vertex's degree is dominated by the depths at its\ntwo endpoints, so the maximum-degree vertex is always incident to a near-deepest point; the\nheuristic therefore tracks the geometric congestion it is meant to relieve. Why now? With the\noverlap law pinned down, \"degree\" and \"depth\" become quantitatively comparable, making a\nformal approximation ratio attainable rather than merely empirical.\n\n## 3. SSA destruction preserves perfectness up to a bounded blow-up\n\n**Conjecture.** Converting an SSA program out of SSA form (inserting copy/move instructions at\n\u03c6-nodes) can increase the maximum overlap by at most the maximum \u03c6-arity, and the resulting\ninterference graph remains perfect whenever the copies are coalesced along a chordal schedule.\n\nThe key insight is that \u03c6-node resolution only splices intervals at basic-block boundaries, an\noperation that refines rather than tangles the interval structure, so chordality \u2014 and hence\nthe `\u03c7 = \u03c9` guarantee \u2014 survives. Why now? The interval model makes SSA destruction an\nexplicit interval-splicing operation, so its effect on the overlap profile can be bounded\nexactly instead of estimated.\n\n## 4. The overlap law fails exactly at the first non-interval obstruction\n\n**Conjecture.** An interference graph satisfies `\u03c7 = maxDepth` for *every* live-range\nassignment realising it if and only if it is an interval graph; the minimal obstructions are\nthe induced cycles of length \u2265 4 and the asteroidal triples.\n\nThe key insight is that `\u03c7 = \u03c9` is the perfect-graph signature, and the extra geometric\nidentity `\u03c9 = maxDepth` is precisely the interval-representability signature, so the two\ntogether characterise interval graphs rather than the strictly larger class of chordal graphs.\nWhy now? Having separated the perfectness bound from the overlap identity in this cycle, the\ngap between them can be probed with the known forbidden-subgraph catalogue for interval graphs.\n\n## 5. Register pressure is a submodular potential\n\n**Conjecture.** The map sending a set `S` of variables to the maximum overlap of the induced\nsub-configuration is submodular, so the deepest congested regions can be identified, and\nrelieved, by matroid-greedy optimisation with provable optimality.\n\nThe key insight is that maximum overlap is a max of sums of indicator functions, and such\n\"height of a union of intervals\" functionals are submodular in the underlying interval set.\nWhy now? The clean identity between overlap and chromatic number lets submodular optimisation\nspeak directly about register counts, connecting compiler register allocation to the\nwell-developed theory of submodular minimisation.\n",
