    "description": "# Future Directions \u2014 The Caterer Hierarchy and Higher Layers\n\nThis cycle established the two lowest floors of the *caterer hierarchy*: the lazy caterer\nnumbers (plane regions from lines) and the cake numbers (space regions from planes), together\nwith the layer recurrence `c(n+1) = c n + p n` that promotes one dimension to the next by\naccumulating a single binomial column. The following conjectures push that structure upward\nand sideways.\n\n## Conjecture 1 \u2014 The full dimensional tower is a Pascal prefix\n\nLet `H_d(n) = C(n,0) + C(n,1) + \u22ef + C(n,d)` be the maximal number of regions into which\n`d`-dimensional space is divided by `n` hyperplanes in general position. Then every consecutive\npair satisfies the layer recurrence `H_{d}(n+1) = H_{d}(n) + H_{d-1}(n)`, and each `H_d` is a\ndegree-`d` quasi-polynomial with leading term `n^d / d!`.\n\n*The key insight is* that \"adding one hyperplane in dimension `d`\" and \"cutting the arrangement\none dimension down\" are the same combinatorial act, so the entire tower is generated by a single\nPascal-triangle prefix rather than by `d` unrelated closed forms.\n\n*Why now?* The base cases `d = 2` (lazy caterer) and `d = 3` (cake) are now settled with the\nlayer recurrence isolated as the driving mechanism; the induction on `d` needs only Pascal's\nrule, which the current framework already exercises.\n\n## Conjecture 2 \u2014 A sharp periodic parity law across the hierarchy\n\nFor each dimension `d`, the parity of `H_d(n)` is a purely periodic function of `n` with period\n`2^{\u2308log\u2082(d+1)\u2309\u207a}` (a power of two), and the density of odd values converges to a dyadic\nrational determined by `d`.\n\n*The key insight is* that parity of a truncated Pascal row is governed by Lucas' theorem on the\nbinary digits of `n`, so the seemingly arithmetic \"odd iff `n mod 4 \u2208 {0,3}`\" law for the plane\nis the `d = 2` shadow of a uniform base-2 phenomenon.\n\n*Why now?* The `d = 2` parity law `caterer_odd_iff` is proved and exhibits period 4; matching it\nto the binary-digit prediction gives an immediately testable base case for the general statement.\n\n## Conjecture 3 \u2014 Partial sums climb the tower by exactly one floor\n\nThe running totals of one floor are an affine shift of the next floor:\n`\u2211_{k\u2264n} H_d(k) = (n+1) + [H_{d+1}(n) \u2212 1]`, generalizing the tetrahedral partial-sum identity\n`\u2211_{k\u2264n} p k = (n+1) + C(n+2,3)`.\n\n*The key insight is* that summation acts as an integration operator that raises the Pascal prefix\nlength by one while contributing a constant column, so partial summation and dimensional lifting\nare inverse-adjacent operations on the hierarchy.\n\n*Why now?* The `d = 2` instance `caterer_partial_sum` is verified, pinning down the constant\n`(n+1)` term and the `C(n+2,3)` lift; the general claim is a clean induction target.\n\n## Conjecture 4 \u2014 Extremal rigidity of the region count\n\nAmong all arrangements of `n` hyperplanes in `d`-space, the value `H_d(n)` is attained **only** by\narrangements in general position, and any degeneracy strictly lowers the count by an amount equal\nto a sum of lower-hierarchy terms `H_{d-2}` indexed by the degenerate flats.\n\n*The key insight is* that each coincidence between hyperplanes removes precisely the pieces a\nlower-dimensional sub-arrangement would have created, so defect from the maximum is itself a\ncaterer-hierarchy quantity.\n\n*Why now?* With the maxima `H_2, H_3` and their recurrence in hand, the defect terms can be\npredicted exactly, turning a qualitative \"general position is best\" heuristic into a quantitative,\nfalsifiable formula.\n",
