    "description": "# Future Directions\n\n## What is proved here\n\n`TransfiniteComputation.lean` establishes a machine-checked **bridge** between\ncellular-automaton dynamics and ordinal / transfinite computation, via the theory of\nmonotone operators and their ordinal-approximated least fixed points\n(`OrdinalApprox.lfpApprox`).\n\nConcretely, for the spreading cellular automaton `spread : Set \u2115 \u2192o Set \u2115`\n(source at `0`, activity spreads one cell per step):\n\n- `mem_spread` \u2014 the rule is genuinely **local** (radius 1).\n- `spread_iterate` \u2014 finite-time evolution: `spread^[k] \u2205 = Iio k`.\n- `finite_stage_not_univ` \u2014 **no finite stage** completes the computation.\n- `spread_lfp` \u2014 the least fixed point is the all-on configuration `univ`.\n- `lfpApprox_nat` \u2014 ordinal evolution agrees with iteration at finite stages.\n- `lfpApprox_omega_eq_iUnion` \u2014 the **limit (ITTM `liminf`) rule** at `\u03c9` is the union\n  of all finite stages.\n- `spread_reaches_univ_at_omega` / `omega_stage_eq_lfp` \u2014 the computation **completes\n  exactly at the transfinite stage `\u03c9`**, reaching `spread.lfp`.\n- `transfinite_computation` \u2014 the headline connector: complete at `\u03c9`, never at any\n  finite time (closure ordinal exactly `\u03c9`).\n- `ca_fixed_point_is_transfinitely_computable` \u2014 the abstract bridge: for *any*\n  monotone CA rule on *any* complete-lattice configuration space, the global fixed\n  point is a value of the transfinite ordinal evolution.\n\nThis captures the core sense in which cellular automata \"run at the ordinals\" strictly\nexceed their finite-time counterparts, paralleling how Infinite Time Turing Machines\nexceed ordinary Turing machines: the limit rule (take the union / `liminf`) is exactly\nwhat a monotone run does at limit stages.\n\n## Natural next steps\n\n1. **Closure ordinals beyond `\u03c9`.**  Design monotone CA rules whose least fixed point is\n   only reached at `\u03c9+1`, `\u03c9\u00b72`, `\u03c9\u00b2`, \u2026 and prove the exact closure ordinal. A rule on\n   `Set (\u2115 \u00d7 \u2115)` that fills row `i+1` only once row `i` is complete has closure ordinal\n   `\u03c9\u00b2`, matching the mission's \"`\u03c9\u00b2`\" target. The lattice-theoretic scaffolding\n   (`lfpApprox_add_one`, `lfpApprox_monotone`, `lt_omega0`) used here generalizes\n   directly; the work is computing the limit stages `\u2a06 b < \u03bb \u2026` at each limit `\u03bb`.\n\n2. **A Rule-110 analog.**  Rule 110 itself is *not* monotone, so the least-fixed-point\n   machinery does not apply verbatim. Two routes: (a) study a monotone *sub-shift* /\n   growth restriction of Rule-110-like dynamics where `lfpApprox` applies; or (b) model\n   the genuinely non-monotone limit dynamics with the ITTM `liminf` rule directly\n   (`Filter.liminf` of the `Bool`-valued cell traces) and prove a simulation result.\n\n3. **ITTM connection, formalized.**  Define an Infinite Time Turing Machine tape as\n   `\u2124 \u2192 Bool` evolving over `Ordinal` with the `liminf` limit rule, and prove that the\n   monotone CA studied here is *simulated* by such a machine, so that the CA's closure\n   ordinal is an ITTM halting time. `lfpApprox_omega_eq_iUnion` is the first step: it\n   identifies the CA limit rule with the ITTM limit rule for monotone runs.\n\n4. **Super-Turing separation.**  Exhibit a set decidable by a transfinite CA but not by\n   any finite-time CA (a diagonal / halting-style argument), giving a formal\n   super-Turing statement. The present `finite_stage_not_univ` \u2227\n   `spread_reaches_univ_at_omega` is a clean toy separation (the predicate \"all cells\n   eventually on\") that a finite-time run cannot certify but a transfinite run can.\n\n5. **Least vs. greatest fixed points.**  Dualize using `gfpApprox` to model\n   coinductive / \"eventually stable\" CA behaviours and relate to `liminf`/`limsup`\n   asymmetries in ITTM computation.\n",
