    "description": "# Future Directions \u2014 Coarse block\u2013cut tree-decompositions\n\nThis cycle isolated the metric engine behind adhesion-diameter estimates in coarse\ntree-decompositions: distances across a decomposition accumulate linearly along chains of\noverlapping bounded-diameter bags, with the accumulation constant being exactly the quantity\nthat the `5d+2 \u2192 4d+2` question tries to sharpen. The following conjectures are the natural\nnext targets.\n\n## 1. The `4d+2` adhesion optimum via routing-vertex selection\n\n**Conjecture.** For every `d`, every connected graph admits a tree-decomposition in which each\nbag is `(d, 2d+1)`-inseparable and every adhesion set has diameter at most `4d+2`.\n\nThe key insight is that the excess in the previous `5d+2` bound comes from routing the\ndistance estimate through an *arbitrary* shared vertex of two consecutive bags; choosing the\nshared vertex that is metrically central to the adhesion removes one additive `d`, collapsing\n`5d+2` to `4d+2`.\n\nWhy now? The linear-accumulation law has been cleanly separated from the separator theory, so\nthe entire problem is now a *local optimisation over the routing vertex* \u2014 a finite, testable\nquestion about a single adhesion set rather than a global argument about the whole tree.\n\n## 2. Tightness: an unavoidable `4d+2` family\n\n**Conjecture.** There is a family of connected graphs on which *every* tree-decomposition with\n`(d, 2d+1)`-inseparable bags has some adhesion set of diameter at least `4d+1`.\n\nThe key insight is that inseparability forces adjacent bags to overlap in a set that already\nspans two \"radius-`d` shells\", so the adhesion cannot be metrically thinner than roughly `4d`\nwithout violating the thickness requirement.\n\nWhy now? Small-case computation (see the evidence notes) shows subdivided-clique gadgets whose\nadhesions resist compression, giving a concrete blueprint for a matching lower bound.\n\n## 3. Chain-length independence of the additive slack\n\n**Conjecture.** In any tree-decomposition with bags of diameter `\u2264 D`, the distance between a\nvertex of the first bag and a vertex of the `n`-th bag along a tree path is at most\n`(n+1)\u00b7D + n`, and this linear form (slope `D+1`, no super-linear correction) is best possible.\n\nThe key insight is that each additional overlapping bag contributes at most `D` from its own\ndiameter plus `1` from the metric cost of the hand-off, and these contributions never interact\nto produce a super-linear term.\n\nWhy now? The accumulation lemma is fully established, so the remaining question \u2014 matching the\nslope `D+1` with a lower-bound family \u2014 is a concrete extremal problem.\n\n## 4. From block\u2013cut trees to coarse block\u2013cut trees\n\n**Conjecture.** The classical block\u2013cut tree of a connected graph refines into a coarse\ntree-decomposition whose adhesion sets are single \"coarse cutvertices\" of diameter at most\n`2d`, strictly below the general `4d+2` bound.\n\nThe key insight is that genuine cut structure concentrates the adhesion onto a bounded ball\naround a cutvertex, so the generic two-shell overlap degenerates to one shell.\n\nWhy now? Isolating adhesion diameter as the sole metric obstruction makes it possible to ask\nexactly when the block\u2013cut skeleton already delivers the optimal constant, separating the\n\"tree-like\" part of a graph from its dense parts.\n\n## 5. Stability of the bound under bounded-degree perturbation\n\n**Conjecture.** Adding or deleting a bounded number of edges at each vertex changes the optimal\nadhesion-diameter constant by an additive `O(1)` independent of `d`.\n\nThe key insight is that bounded-degree perturbations move graph distances by a bounded additive\namount, and the accumulation law depends on distances only through the bag-diameter parameter.\n\nWhy now? The reduction of the whole theory to a single Lipschitz-in-distance accumulation lemma\nmakes robustness statements of this kind directly attackable.\n",
