    "description": "# Future Directions \u2014 Balancing ratios of circular partitions\n\nThis cycle established two structural pillars for the balancing ratio sequence of\na cyclic partition: windowing never worsens balance (`\u03bc^r \u2264 \u03bc^1`), and balance\nis *exactly perfect* at every multiple of the period (`\u03bc^{kp} = 1`). Together\nthey explain why the balancing ratio must be measured as a `limsup` \u2014 the ratio\nsequence oscillates, resetting to `1` infinitely often. The following\nconjectures push toward the full `2r/p + 1` upper bound.\n\n## Conjecture 1 \u2014 The recipe upper bound\n\nFor every finite recipe of length `p` and every window length `r \u2265 1`, the\nbalancing ratio satisfies `\u03bc_r \u2264 2r/p + 1`.\n\nThe key insight is that between two consecutive \"resets\" to perfect balance, a\nwindow can absorb at most a bounded surplus of oversized arcs, and that surplus\nscales like `r/p` because each full period contributes exactly the average. Why\nnow? With perfect-balance recurrence at multiples of the period already proved,\nthe problem reduces to controlling the *excursions between resets*, a finite and\ntractable per-period estimate rather than an asymptotic mystery.\n\n## Conjecture 2 \u2014 Sharpness of the window envelope\n\nFor every non-uniform partition there exists a window length `r` with\n`1 < \u03bc^r < \u03bc^1`; moreover the envelope `\u03bc^r \u2264 \u03bc^1` is attained only at `r = 1`.\n\nThe key insight is that any strict inequality among arc lengths is smoothed, but\nnever fully erased, by partial-period windows, so the single-gap ratio is the\nunique maximizer of the window-ratio profile. Why now? The proved monotone\nenvelope and the exact period resets bracket the profile from above and below,\nleaving only the strictness to be quantified.\n\n## Conjecture 3 \u2014 Optimal recipes approach the lower envelope\n\nAmong all recipes of length `p`, the minimum achievable `limsup_r \u03bc_r` decreases\nmonotonically to `1` as `p \u2192 \u221e`, at rate `\u0398(1/p)`.\n\nThe key insight is that longer recipes distribute the unavoidable imbalance over\nmore arcs, so the worst per-window surplus shrinks proportionally to the period.\nWhy now? The `k\u00b7period` reset identity gives an exact zero-error subsequence for\nevery recipe, turning the optimization into a clean rate question about the gaps\nbetween resets.\n\n## Conjecture 4 \u2014 Discrepancy bridge\n\nThe balancing ratio `\u03bc_r` of a partition is controlled by the star-discrepancy\n`D_n` of the generating point sequence via `\u03bc_r - 1 = \u0398(r \u00b7 D_n)` along the\nstages `n` realizing the `limsup`.\n\nThe key insight is that the maximum-versus-minimum window imbalance is precisely\na two-sided discrepancy of `r`-fold consecutive sums, linking cake balancing to\nclassical equidistribution theory. Why now? The window-sum framework developed\nhere expresses `\u03bc^r_n` directly as a ratio of extremal partial sums, exactly the\nobject star-discrepancy bounds.\n",
