    "description": "# Future Directions\n\n## Computational Complexity of Alien Civilizations\n\nThis project isolates the *substrate-independent* core of computational\nobstructions: **Lawvere's fixed-point theorem**. Every classical impossibility\nresult \u2014 Cantor's theorem, undecidability of the halting problem, Kleene's\nrecursion theorem, G\u00f6del's first incompleteness theorem, Tarski's undefinability\nof truth, Russell's paradox \u2014 is one instance, obtained by choosing the answer\ntype `B` and the endofunction `f : B \u2192 B`. Because the theorem mentions no\nmachine model, bits, or physics, any civilization able to speak of functions is\nforced to rediscover the same structure. That is the precise sense in which\n\"computational complexity is discovered, not invented\".\n\n### What is proved (see the `.lean` files)\n\n* **`Lawvere.lean`** \u2014 Lawvere's fixed-point theorem (`lawvere`); its\n  contrapositive (`no_pointSurjective_of_fixpointFree`); Cantor's theorem in\n  Boolean form (`cantor_bool`); the strict `Bool`-power step\n  (`cantor_pow`: an injection `A \u21aa A\u2192Bool` but no surjection). Axiom-free core.\n* **`Uncomputability.lean`** \u2014 an abstract `ComputationModel` with no structural\n  assumptions; abstract undecidability (`diagonal_not_realized`), the\n  model-independence statement (`substrate_independent`), and the **oracle /\n  hypercomputation barrier** (`hypercomputation_barrier`): even with an arbitrary,\n  possibly non-computable oracle, no oracle-program decides the model's own jump.\n* **`Hierarchy.lean`** \u2014 the universal complexity hierarchy `Level n` with strict\n  steps (`strict_step`, `mk_lt`), monotonic cardinality (`mk_strictMono`), and no\n  maximal level (`no_maximal`).\n* **`Recursion.lean`** \u2014 the *positive* face: Kleene's recursion theorem\n  (`recursion_theorem`) and quines (`exists_quine`) in complete systems, together\n  with the duality `no_complete_semantics` (a system complete for its program\n  transformations is never complete for its own Boolean decisions).\n\n### Directions to extend\n\n1. **P vs NP as structure, not model.** Formalize abstract resource-bounded\n   classes as closure operators on decision behaviours and state P-vs-NP-type\n   separations as properties of the closure lattice. The relativization results\n   here (`hypercomputation_barrier`) are the seed of a Baker\u2013Gill\u2013Solovay-style\n   theorem: build oracles `A, B` with `P^A = NP^A` and `P^B \u2260 NP^B`, proving\n   diagonalization alone cannot settle P vs NP \u2014 a genuinely substrate-independent\n   meta-theorem.\n\n2. **A concrete Time/Space Hierarchy Theorem.** Instantiate the abstract\n   `Level` tower with an explicit resource measure (step counts of a universal\n   simulator) and prove `DTIME(f) \u228a DTIME(g)` for `f = o(g / log g)` via the same\n   diagonal, connecting the cardinal hierarchy to an honest complexity hierarchy.\n\n3. **Kleene's recursion theorem, computable category.** Replace the (Set-only\n   degenerate) point-surjectivity hypothesis with an *acceptable programming\n   system* built on `Nat.Partrec` / `Computable`, giving a non-degenerate\n   `recursion_theorem` and genuine quines over `\u2115`.\n\n4. **G\u00f6del and Tarski from the same lemma.** Instantiate `lawvere` with the\n   provability/truth predicate to derive incompleteness and undefinability,\n   exhibiting them as literally the same theorem as the halting problem.\n\n5. **Hypercomputation ordinal hierarchy.** Iterate the jump `jump` transfinitely\n   (the arithmetical/hyperarithmetical hierarchy) and show the barrier persists at\n   every level and limit \u2014 formalizing \"even infinite-time / oracle civilizations\n   face analogous barriers\" as a well-founded transfinite recursion.\n",
